      "diagnosis": {
        "analysis": {
          "alternative": "Correction could improve assignments or prevent memory contamination; the small observed guard effect could also reflect a guard near one, limited sensitivity, or cancellation across switch directions. Other retained machinery could explain the advantage over the mixture.",
          "expected_evidence": "A reproducible guarded-minus-no-correction advantage would weigh against removal; a guarded-minus-unguarded switch benefit of at least one percentage point would support the guard. A no-correction advantage would favor removal, while narrow intervals excluding meaningful benefits in either direction would support practical equivalence. Compare each variant with the mixture against the two-percentage-point margin; assignment and memory diagnostics would help assess contamination without proving it.",
          "followup_test": "Run one preregistered paired ablation on fresh development seeds and varied switch intervals: compare the default no-correction candidate with cloned guarded and unguarded correction controls and the fixed mixture on identical streams. Report seed-level paired intervals for each switch direction, stable accuracy and frozen B recall, plus assignment accuracy using synthetic context labels, memory error and correction magnitude near switches. Implement in toolkit/src/agent_candidate.rs and toolkit/tests/agent_generated.rs; the supervisor has not run this follow-up.",
          "hypothesis": "Removing retrospective correction preserves or improves switching and retention because correction contributes little useful assignment information or contaminates memories; its guard may likewise add little benefit.",
          "limitations": "The operational pass compares the whole candidate with the mixture, not the causal effect of correction or the guard. Existing follow-up output is agent-authored, exploratory and uses visible synthetic families; trials within a seed are dependent. Inline sizes exclude heap allocations and timings are noisy, so actual peak memory and matched-budget costs remain unresolved.",
          "observation": "The completed evaluation met all mixture-relative mean thresholds on 20 seeds; similar_rules was essentially tied. The 100-seed development ablation shows condition-dependent effects: in high-noise complementary streams of length 200, no correction exceeded guarded correction in B recall by 0.843 percentage points, while its low-noise stable accuracy was 0.03 points lower. The reported low-noise switch guard averaged about 0.991, with guarded versus unguarded accuracy differing by only 0.005 points. The candidate used 312 inline bytes versus the mixture's 72 and roughly 4.3 times its reported training time."
        },
