      "idea": {
        "approach": "Implemented a candidate that removes retrospective correction while retaining the seeded memory and routing machinery. Added a 100-seed paired development ablation against guarded correction, unguarded correction, and the fixed mixture, with switch, stable, and frozen B-recall measurements, seed-level bootstrap intervals, and correction diagnostics. The required offline tests passed.",
        "falsification": "A reproducible advantage for guarded correction over no correction would weigh against removing it; a guarded-minus-unguarded switch benefit of at least one percentage point would support the guard. The candidate also fails the campaign criterion if any required mean accuracy is more than two percentage points below the mixture.",
        "hypothesis": "The seeded correction and its guard may add little useful information or contaminate memories, so removing them could preserve or improve accuracy while simplifying learning. Alternatively, correction may improve assignments and the guard may prevent cross-switch contamination; the matched controls distinguish their combined effects on prediction.",
        "limitations": "In the first development replay, no-correction versus guarded differences were small and condition-dependent; this does not establish generalization or the supervisor outcome. The guard and correction remain approximate, visible synthetic families permit overfitting, bootstrap intervals are exploratory, and accuracy alone cannot identify assignment errors. Fresh supervisor mixture comparisons have not been run.",
        "question": "Does removing retrospective correction improve retention and switching relative to the seeded guarded and unguarded variants while remaining competitive with the fixed mixture?"
